From: Andrew Clunis <andrew@orospakr.ca>
Subject: [PATCH] Input: synaptics-rmi4 - let the device settle after the PS/2 reset on SMBus resume

On resume, psmouse_smbus_reconnect() deactivates the PS/2 side of an
SMBus-attached Synaptics touchpad before rmi_smb_resume() runs.  On
these devices that PS/2 command resets the touchpad: it comes back with
its firmware defaults (interrupt mask cleared, doze interval back to the
default), which is why rmi_smb_resume() has to run the configuration
pass again.

Running that pass within a few tens of milliseconds of the reset leaves
the device misbehaving until the next reset, in one of two ways:

 - the report clock drops from a flat 15 ms to 12.5 ms with merged
   23-24 ms frames (roughly half of the resumes), or
 - the first report of every touch is stale: the frame at touch-down
   carries the landing position and the next frame, 15 ms later,
   already carries wherever the finger has moved to, several
   millimetres away, so the cursor sits still after touch-down and then
   jumps.

Measured on a ThinkPad T14 Gen 2a (LEN2073, TM3471-030), issuing the
configuration pass a fixed time after the reset, in place and across
4-minute S3 cycles:

  delay before configuring    stale first frame (median)   drags from rest
        0 ms                        91 units                    0/7
       30 ms                        97 units                    0/6
      100 ms                        91 units                    0/7
      300 ms                         0                          7/7
      300 ms, across S3              0, flat 15 ms clock        7/7
     2000 ms                         0                          7/7

Wait 300 ms before reconfiguring the device on resume.

Signed-off-by: Andrew Clunis <andrew@orospakr.ca>
---
 drivers/input/rmi4/rmi_smbus.c | 14 ++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/input/rmi4/rmi_smbus.c b/drivers/input/rmi4/rmi_smbus.c
--- a/drivers/input/rmi4/rmi_smbus.c
+++ b/drivers/input/rmi4/rmi_smbus.c
@@ -19,6 +19,8 @@
 
 #define SMB_PROTOCOL_VERSION_ADDRESS	0xfd
 #define SMB_MAX_COUNT			32
+/* time the device needs after the PS/2 reset it gets on resume before it accepts configuration */
+#define RMI_SMB_RESUME_SETTLE_MS	300
 #define RMI_SMB2_MAP_SIZE		8 /* 8 entry of 4 bytes each */
 #define RMI_SMB2_MAP_FLAGS_WE		0x01
 
@@ -380,6 +382,18 @@
 
 	rmi_smb_reset(&rmi_smb->xport, 0);
 
+	/*
+	 * The PS/2 side of the device has just been deactivated by
+	 * psmouse_smbus_reconnect(), and on Synaptics touchpads that resets
+	 * the device: it comes back with its firmware defaults and needs the
+	 * configuration below again. Configuring it too soon after that reset
+	 * leaves it misbehaving until the next reset - either an 80 Hz report
+	 * clock with merged frames, or a stale first frame on every touch (the
+	 * landing position, followed 15 ms later by wherever the finger has
+	 * moved to since). It needs a few hundred milliseconds.
+	 */
+	msleep(RMI_SMB_RESUME_SETTLE_MS);
+
 	rmi_reset(rmi_dev);
 
 	ret = rmi_driver_resume(rmi_smb->xport.rmi_dev, true);
